      <h1 id="stage-12-glossary" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 12: Glossary<a href="#stage-12-glossary" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Add a glossary and link terms in the guide to their definitions. A <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> defines one term and can include abbreviations or synonyms. A <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> contains several entries in one file.</p>
      <p>Define a key for each entry. Use <code>&lt;term keyref&gt;</code> to link a term to its entry and <code>&lt;abbreviated-form keyref&gt;</code> to let the processor choose the expanded or abbreviated form.</p>
      <p><strong>Optional module.</strong> See <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path">Choose a learning path</a> for the starting checkpoint and the next core lesson.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 30 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 11 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded output below is an example. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/run-the-gate">Check your work</a> to see the result for your own project.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-write-the-entries" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: Write the entries<a href="#step-1-write-the-entries" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/glossary/</code><strong> and the six entry files</strong> Each is a <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> with the glossary entry DOCTYPE: a <code>&lt;glossterm&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code>, nothing else.
      <p>In the <strong>Explorer</strong>, right-click the <code>topics</code> folder, choose <strong>New Folder</strong>, and enter <code>glossary</code>. Right-click the <code>glossary</code> folder, choose <strong>New File</strong>, enter <code>g-sample-rate.dita</code>, and choose the <strong>Glossary Entry</strong> template:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"g-sample-rate\">\n  <glossterm>Term</glossterm>\n  <glossdef></glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" />
      <p>The editor sets the <code>@id</code> from the file name, <code>g-sample-rate</code>. This lesson uses the <code>gl-</code> prefix for glossary ids and keys, so change the id to <code>gl-sample-rate</code>. Replace <code>Term</code> with <strong>XML</strong> &gt; <strong>Select Element Content</strong>, and type the definition between <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;/glossdef&gt;</code>. Create the other five files in the same way, and change each id from <code>g-</code> to <code>gl-</code>, for example <code>g-clipping</code> to <code>gl-clipping</code>. If you use another editor, create the folder and the files, and type the finished listings. The finished entries:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"gl-sample-rate\">\n  <glossterm>Sample rate</glossterm>\n  <glossdef>The number of audio samples captured per second, measured in hertz. Higher sample rates capture more detail. CD audio uses 44,100 Hz; video production typically uses 48,000 Hz.</glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"gl-bit-depth\">\n  <glossterm>Bit depth</glossterm>\n  <glossdef>The number of bits used to store each sample, which sets how precisely amplitude is recorded. 16-bit audio has 65,536 possible values; 24-bit has over 16 million.</glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"gl-clipping\">\n  <glossterm>Clipping</glossterm>\n  <glossdef>Distortion that occurs when a signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent. Clipped peaks are cut flat and cannot be repaired.</glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"gl-normalization\">\n  <glossterm>Normalization</glossterm>\n  <glossdef>Raising or lowering the level of a recording so that its loudest peak sits at a chosen target, giving consistent loudness across clips.</glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"gl-compression\">\n  <glossterm>Compression</glossterm>\n  <glossdef>Reducing the dynamic range of audio so that quiet parts are louder and loud parts quieter. Not to be confused with file compression such as MP3.</glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-compression.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n<glossentry id=\"gl-waveform\">\n  <glossterm>Waveform</glossterm>\n  <glossdef>The shape of a sound wave over time, drawn as amplitude against time. Audacity shows each track as a waveform.</glossdef>\n</glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/glossary/audio-units.dita</code> A <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> with two entries whose terms have abbreviations.
      <p>Create <code>audio-units.dita</code> in the <code>glossary</code> folder, and choose the <strong>Glossary Group</strong> template:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossgroup PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Group//EN\" \"glossgroup.dtd\">\n\n<glossgroup id=\"audio-units\">\n  <title>Glossary</title>\n  <glossentry id=\"term\">\n    <glossterm>Term</glossterm>\n    <glossdef></glossdef>\n  </glossentry>\n</glossgroup>"} lang="xml" />
      <p>The group's <code>@id</code> comes from the file name and needs no editing. Replace the title with <code>Units</code>. The sample entry keeps the id <code>term</code>, so change it to <code>gl-decibel</code>, fill in the entry, and add its <code>&lt;glossBody&gt;</code>. Then add the <code>gl-hertz</code> entry after it. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing. The finished group:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE glossgroup PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Group//EN\" \"glossgroup.dtd\">\n\n<glossgroup id=\"audio-units\">\n  <title>Units</title>\n  <glossentry id=\"gl-decibel\">\n    <glossterm>decibel</glossterm>\n    <glossdef>A logarithmic unit for the ratio between two levels. In audio editing, 0 dB is the loudest level a file can hold, and quieter sounds are negative.</glossdef>\n    <glossBody>\n      <glossSurfaceForm>decibel (dB)</glossSurfaceForm>\n      <glossAlt>\n        <glossAbbreviation>dB</glossAbbreviation>\n      </glossAlt>\n    </glossBody>\n  </glossentry>\n  <glossentry id=\"gl-hertz\">\n    <glossterm>hertz</glossterm>\n    <glossdef>Cycles per second. Sample rates are given in hertz: 44,100 Hz means 44,100 samples every second.</glossdef>\n    <glossBody>\n      <glossSurfaceForm>hertz (Hz)</glossSurfaceForm>\n      <glossAlt>\n        <glossAbbreviation>Hz</glossAbbreviation>\n      </glossAlt>\n      <glossAlt>\n        <glossSynonym>cycles per second</glossSynonym>\n      </glossAlt>\n    </glossBody>\n  </glossentry>\n</glossgroup>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/audio-units.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> is a topic: it has an <code>@id</code>, and <code>&lt;glossterm&gt;</code> takes the place of <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>. <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code> is the definition, a short paragraph.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> is also a topic, with its own DOCTYPE, <code>@id</code> and <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, containing <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> topics. In stage 13 the metadata policy treats it as a topic in its own right, so it gets a prolog like any other.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;glossBody&gt;</code> holds everything beyond the definition. <code>&lt;glossSurfaceForm&gt;</code> is how the term is written the first time a reader meets it, "decibel (dB)". Each <code>&lt;glossAlt&gt;</code> is one alternative form: a <code>&lt;glossAbbreviation&gt;</code> or a <code>&lt;glossSynonym&gt;</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-key-the-entries-and-put-them-in-the-guide" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: Key the entries and put them in the guide<a href="#step-2-key-the-entries-and-put-them-in-the-guide" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>keydefs-glossary.ditamap</code> Create the file in <code>my-audacity-guide</code> from the <strong>Key Definition Map</strong> template, as in stage 10. Replace the title with <code>Glossary key definitions</code>, and replace the two sample key definitions with the eight glossary keys. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing.
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Glossary key definitions</title>\n\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-sample-rate\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-bit-depth\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-clipping\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-normalization\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-compression\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-waveform\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-decibel\" href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita#gl-decibel\"/>\n  <keydef keys=\"gl-hertz\" href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita#gl-hertz\"/>\n</map>"} lang="xml" title="keydefs-glossary.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code> The keydef map joins the key space by <code>&lt;mapref&gt;</code>, and a <em>Glossary</em> heading puts the group and the six entries in the table of contents.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n+++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@\n   </topicmeta>\n \n   <mapref href=\"keydefs-product.ditamap\"/>\n+  <mapref href=\"keydefs-glossary.ditamap\"/>\n   <keydef keys=\"start-here\" href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n   <!-- Warehouses: pulled in by conref and conkeyref, never pages of their own. -->\n   <keydef keys=\"common-notes\" href=\"shared/common-notes.dita\"/>\n@@ -37,6 +38,16 @@\n       </topicmeta>\n     </topicref>\n   </topichead>\n+  <topichead navtitle=\"Glossary\">\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita\"/>\n+    <topicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita\"/>\n+  </topichead>\n+\n   <reltable>\n     <title>Concept, task and reference links</title>\n     <relheader>"} lang="diff" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the keys</strong>
      <ul>
      <li>A key per entry, named <code>gl-…</code> so a glance at a <code>@keyref</code> says it is a glossary key.</li>
      <li>The two entries inside the group are keyed with a fragment, <code>audio-units.dita#gl-decibel</code>: the key resolves to the <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> element by its <code>@id</code>, not to the group. That is what lets an <code>&lt;abbreviated-form&gt;</code> find the surface form of the right entry.</li>
      <li>The entries are ordinary topics in the map. DITA-OT publishes them as pages under <code>out/full/topics/glossary/</code>, and links from the text land on them.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-3-bind-the-terms-in-the-text" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: Bind the terms in the text<a href="#step-3-bind-the-terms-in-the-text" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit the three topics</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@\n     If you would rather start recording, go straight to <xref href=\"recording-your-first-track.dita\"/> and come back later.</p>\n     <section>\n       <title>Waveforms</title>\n-      <p>Sound travels through air as a continuous <term>waveform</term> of pressure changes.\n+      <p>Sound travels through air as a continuous <term keyref=\"gl-waveform\">waveform</term> of pressure changes.\n       A microphone converts these pressure changes into an electrical signal.\n       To store the signal digitally, the computer takes thousands of measurements, called <term>samples</term>, of the signal's amplitude every second.</p>\n       <fig id=\"fig-waveform\">\n@@ -34,7 +34,7 @@\n     </section>\n     <section>\n       <title>Sample rate</title>\n-      <p>The <term>sample rate</term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in hertz (Hz).\n+      <p>The <term keyref=\"gl-sample-rate\">sample rate</term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in <abbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-hertz\"/>.\n       Common sample rates are:</p>\n       <sl>\n         <sli>44,100 Hz: CD quality, enough for most audio work</sli>\n@@ -45,7 +45,7 @@\n     </section>\n     <section>\n       <title>Bit depth</title>\n-      <p><term>Bit depth</term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.</p>\n+      <p><term keyref=\"gl-bit-depth\">Bit depth</term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.</p>\n       <simpletable>\n         <sthead>\n           <stentry>Bit depth</stentry>\n@@ -75,7 +75,7 @@\n         <dlentry>\n           <dt>Amplitude</dt>\n           <dd>The height of a sound wave, corresponding to how loud the sound is.\n-          Measured in decibels (dB), a ratio against a reference level:\n+          Measured in <abbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-decibel\"/>, a ratio against a reference level:\n           <equation-block>\n             <mathml>\n               <m:math xmlns:m=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\">\n@@ -93,7 +93,7 @@\n           A level of <equation-inline><mathml><m:math xmlns:m=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mtext>dB</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:math></mathml><ph>−6 dB</ph></equation-inline> is half the amplitude of the reference.</dd>\n         </dlentry>\n         <dlentry>\n-          <dt>Clipping</dt>\n+          <dt><term keyref=\"gl-clipping\">Clipping</term></dt>\n           <dd>Distortion that occurs when the signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent.\n           It appears as flat-topped waveforms.</dd>\n         </dlentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/preparing-to-record.dita\n+++ b/topics/preparing-to-record.dita\n@@ -10,9 +10,9 @@\n     </context>\n     <steps-unordered>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Speak at normal volume and watch the recording meter; aim for peaks around -12 dB.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Speak at normal volume and watch the recording meter; aim for peaks around -12 <abbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-decibel\"/>.</cmd>\n         <tutorialinfo>\n-          <p>Leaving headroom avoids clipping, the flat-topped distortion that no effect can repair.</p>\n+          <p>Leaving headroom avoids <term keyref=\"gl-clipping\">clipping</term>, the flat-topped distortion that no effect can repair.</p>\n         </tutorialinfo>\n       </step>\n       <step>"} lang="diff" title="topics/preparing-to-record.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n+++ b/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@\n   <refbody>\n     <section>\n       <p><keyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> imports and exports audio in several formats.\n-      <term>Lossless</term> formats keep every sample of the source; <term>lossy</term> formats discard detail to make smaller files.\n+      <term>Lossless</term> formats keep every sample at the recorded <term keyref=\"gl-bit-depth\">bit depth</term> of the source; <term>lossy</term> formats discard detail to make smaller files.\n       Exported files are named after the project, so <filepath>podcast-episode-1.aup3</filepath> exports as <filepath>podcast-episode-1.mp3</filepath>.</p>\n     </section>\n     <section id=\"choosing\">\n@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@\n           </row>\n           <row>\n             <entry>MP3</entry>\n-            <entry>Compressed</entry>\n+            <entry><term keyref=\"gl-compression\">Compressed</term></entry>\n             <entry>Lossy</entry>\n             <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n             Needs the <i>LAME</i> encoder, bundled since version 2.3.2."} lang="diff" title="topics/supported-audio-formats.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the two forms</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;term keyref="gl-clipping"&gt;clipping&lt;/term&gt;</code> keeps the word as written and binds it to the entry. The output makes it a link to the definition, with the <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code> as the link's hover text. The content stays, so "Compressed" in a table cell and "clipping" in a sentence keep their own capitalization and grammar.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;abbreviated-form keyref="gl-decibel"/&gt;</code> is empty. The processor writes the term for you: the <code>&lt;glossSurfaceForm&gt;</code> where the reader first meets it, and the <code>&lt;glossAbbreviation&gt;</code> after that. In the HTML5 output of DITA-OT 4.3.5 each of the two topics that uses it shows <code>decibel (dB)</code>, linked to the entry, and "hertz (Hz)" likewise.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;abbreviated-form&gt;</code> inserts the surface form exactly as the glossary entry writes it, in the singular. The output reads "-12 decibel (dB)" and "Measured in decibel (dB)". For a measurement after a number, where the reader expects "-12 dB", keep the plain unit instead.</li>
      <li>The <code>&lt;term&gt;</code> elements without a <code>@keyref</code>, such as <em>Lossless</em> and <em>lossy</em>, are unchanged: they mark a term but have no entry to link to.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-4-check-your-work" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 4: Check your work<a href="#step-4-check-your-work" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check your work</strong>
      <p>Format the topics: choose <strong>XML</strong> &gt; <strong>Format</strong> in each new or changed topic and save it, or choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Project Tools</strong> &gt; <strong>Format Project</strong> to format every file at once. From the command line, run:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita topics/glossary/*.dita"} lang="bash" />
      <p>Then check the project. In the editor, choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Check Project</strong> and read the result in the <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel. From the command line, run:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml check ."} lang="bash" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   clean, with warnings\n  unused key: start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:12] — nothing references it\n  unused key: digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:19] — nothing references it\n  unused key: gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10] — nothing references it\nbuild    full                 ok  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/out/full\noutput   clean (full)\nReady: the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and the output of full holds together. 3 unused keys above: worth knowing, and not treated as failures."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The project now has twenty-one files: nine topics, two shared topics, seven glossary files and three maps. The unused keys are defined for later stages: stage 14 refers to <code>gl-normalization</code>, and stage 18 uses <code>start-here</code> and <code>digital-audio</code>.</p>
      <p>The check builds the guide into <code>out/full/</code>. Open <code>out/full/topics/glossary/audio-units.html</code>: the group is one page with both entries, each showing the term, the definition, the surface form and the alternative forms.</p>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <p>A glossary key is a key like any other. Change the <code>&lt;term keyref&gt;</code> in <em>Preparing to record</em> to <code>gl-clippng</code> and check your work. The check stops at health. Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   NOT CLEAN\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/preparing-to-record.dita:15  @keyref=\"gl-clippng\" — key 'gl-clippng' not defined\n  (run project-health for the full report)\n  unused key: start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:12] — nothing references it\n  unused key: digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:19] — nothing references it\n  unused key: gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10] — nothing references it\nNot ready: the project itself has faults. The build and the built output were not checked."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel, or <code>dogsbay-xml project-health .</code>, gives the full report:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Root map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nUndefined keys (1):\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/preparing-to-record.dita:15  key 'gl-clippng' not defined\nUnused keys (3):\n  start-here  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:12]\n  digital-audio  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:19]\n  gl-normalization  [/home/you/my-audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10]\n\nSummary\n  Undefined keys                1\n  Unused keys                   3"} lang="plaintext" />
